[Printed statement] [p. 691 (PDF p. 19)] Compared to the setting with no interference, the limit of the variance estimator contains two additional terms. The term $B_1$ captures the consequences of direct interference between the units. That is, it captures whether unit $i$ interferes with unit $j$ directly, in which case $ reve\xi_{ji} 
e 0$. If there is no interference, there are no spillover effects, so $\xi_{ij}(1) = \xi_{ij}(0) = 0$, and $B_1$ is zero. [What is refuted] The implication, for $i 
e j$: if unit $i$ interferes with unit $j$ ($I_{ij} = 1$), then $ reve\xi_{ji} 
e 0$. [Counterexample, from the validator] $n = 3$; $Z_1, Z_2, Z_3$ independent Bernoulli($ rac{1}{2}$); $y_2(\mathbf{z}) = z_1(2z_3 - 1)$ and $y_1(\mathbf{z}) = y_3(\mathbf{z}) = 0$ for every $\mathbf{z} \in \{0, 1\}^3$. All potential outcomes have absolute value at most 1, so Assumption 1 holds with $k = 2$ and every $q$, $s$. Changing $z_1$ from 0 to 1 changes $y_2$ by $2z_3 - 1 
e 0$, so $I_{12} = 1$. For $z \in \{0, 1\}$, $\xi_{21}(z) = \mathrm{E}[y_{21}(z, 1; Z_3) - y_{21}(z, 0; Z_3)] = \mathrm{E}[2Z_3 - 1] = 0$, so $ reve\xi_{21} = (1 - p_2)\xi_{21}(1) + p_2\xi_{21}(0) = 0$. Thus unit 1 interferes with unit 2 directly and $ reve\xi_{21} = 0$.

[Corrected sentences] The term $B_1$ captures contributions from expected direct spillover effects. Direct interference from $i$ to $j$ can give $ reve\xi_{ji} 
e 0$, although assignment-specific spillovers may average to zero. [Formal content] For any design and any distinct $i, j \in \mathbf{U}$ for which the expectations exist: (i) if $I_{ij} = 0$, then $y_{ji}(a, 1; \mathbf{z}_{-ji}) = y_{ji}(a, 0; \mathbf{z}_{-ji})$ for all $a \in \{0, 1\}$ and all $\mathbf{z}_{-ji}$, hence $\xi_{ji}(0) = \xi_{ji}(1) = 0$ and $ reve\xi_{ji} = 0$; equivalently, $ reve\xi_{ji} 
e 0$ implies $I_{ij} = 1$; (ii) the converse fails: $I_{ij} = 1$ does not imply $ reve\xi_{ji} 
e 0$ (the counterexample target for this finding); (iii) as printed: if there is no interference, then $\xi_{ij}(1) = \xi_{ij}(0) = 0$ for all $i 
e j$, and $B_1 = 0$.
